# Untitled

## Flexible Zirconium Metal-Organic Frameworks as Bioinspired Switchable Catalysts
In nature, enzyme activity is often modulated through feedback loops and a variety of trigger-induced effects. Inspired by nature, chemists have been developing catalysts whose activity can be controlled by external stimuli.[1] Such systems are capable of alternating the environment of their active centers, which in turn regulates the reaction rate and selectivity, functioning as allosteric catalysts.[2] Flexible metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are suitable platforms for the development of artificial switchable catalysts in consideration of their inherent cavities and dynamic behaviors.[3] They are capable of responding to various chemical and physical stimuli, such as light, pressure, temperature, or guest molecules.[4] A prominent example is the well-established "breathing behavior", in which the framework experiences a reversible unit-cell dimensional change as a result of host–guest interactions.[5] Different from other porous materials, such as zeolites and activated carbons, flexible MOFs respond to the stimuli with retention of high regularity, which allows
for structure characterization by means of crystallography, therefore maximizing the understanding of correlation between applied stimuli and ensuing properties.[6] Additionally, the inherent cavities and dynamic behaviors of flexible MOFs are reminiscent of sophisticated biological systems, such as regulatory enzymes, in which stimuli induce conformational changes and variations of catalytic activities.
High stability and dynamic pore architecture are indispensable in switchable MOF catalysts. The \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\) cluster, in this regard, is a promising building unit for the construction of switchable MOF catalysts. The dihedral angles between the \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\) clusters and carboxylate linkers vary from 0 to \(14.5^{\circ}\) in various \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -MOFs, suggesting extensive flexibility in the Zrcarboxylate junction.[7] In addition, the chemical inertness of \(Z\mathbf{r}{-}\mathbf{O}\) bonds make the \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -MOFs robust platforms for a variety of catalytic applications.[8] Although \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -MOFs are one of the research focuses in recent years, the reported \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -MOFs usually show limited breathing amplitude that mainly resulted from linker flexibility.[9] This promotes us to develop flexible \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -MOFs for the realization of switchable catalysis.
A \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -MOF with flexible bcu topology, namely PCN-700- \(\mathbf{Me}_{2}\) , was selected as a prototype MOF[12b]. PCN-700-M2 crystallizes in the tetragonal space group \(P4_{2}/m m c\) . Each \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\) cluster is coordinated to eight \(\mathbf{Me}_{2}\) -BPDC linkers \(\mathbf{Me}_{2}\) - \(\mathrm{BPDC}\!=\!2{,}2^{\prime}\) -dimethylsulphenyl- \(\cdot4{,}4^{\prime}\) -dicarboxylate) and eight terminal \(\mathrm{-OH^{-}/H_{2}O}\) groups. The overall framework can be simplified into a flexible bcu net, which is expected to shrink along the \(c\) -axis while expanding within the \(^{a b}\) -plane (Figure S4 in the Supporting Information). Breathing behaviors of MOFs are usually triggered by the removal or introduction of guest molecules. Bearing this in mind, we carried out successive single-crystal X-ray diffraction (SC-XRD) analyses on \(\mathrm{PCN-700-Me}_{2}\) during desolvation to generate "snapshots" of the breathing process. Crystallographic data clearly shows that \(\mathrm{PCN-700-Me}_{2}\) exhibits a "scissor-jack-like" behavior, shrinking along \(c\) -direction by tweaking the metal–linker angle (Figure 1a). A significant decrease in the length of \(c\) - axis (from \(14.92\,\mathrm{\AA}\) to \(11.24\,\mathrm{\AA}\) ) and a slight increase in the length of \(a/b\) -axis (from \(24.35\,\mathrm{\AA}\) to \(24.84\,\mathrm{\AA}\) ) were observed upon guest removal. The flexible \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -carboxylate connection, acting as a hinge, is primarily responsible for the breathing behavior. As shown in Figure 1e,f, \(\mathrm{PCN-700-Me}_{2}\) undergoes a large conformational change that is associated with the bending of \(Z\mathbf{r}\) -O-C angle (from \(133^{\circ}\) to \(130^{\circ}\) ) and, more intuitively, the varying of dihedral angle between the equatorial plane of \(\mathrm{O-Zr-Zr-O}\) and the plane of carboxylate (from \(10^{\circ}\) to \(32^{\circ}\) ). The bending of \(Z\mathbf{r}\) –carboxylate bond affords a closer packing along \(c\) -direction, which gives rise to a shrinkage of the \(c\) -axis. A closer investigation indicates
that the breathing motion of \(\mathsf{P C N-700-M e}_{2}\) is a collective result. Along with the bending of \(Z\mathbf{r}\) –carboxylate bond, we also observed a rotation of the \(\mathbf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{C}\) bond between two phenyl rings, which alleviates the steric hindrance within the structure by arranging the two methyl groups on adjacent linkers as far apart as possible. As a collateral effect of the rotation of \(\mathbf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{C}\) bond, \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\) clusters tit about \(14^{\circ}\) (Figure 1d). An overlap of different conformations during desolvation (Figure 1g) underlines the fact that the bending of \(Z\mathbf{r}-\) carboxylate junction and the conformational change of the organic linker together account for the breathing behavior of \(\mathsf{P C N-700-M e}_{2}\).
It should be noted that the axial breathing amplitude of \(\mathsf{P C N-700-M e}_{2}\) is much larger than the volumetric breathing amplitude. The nearly uniaxial breathing behavior can be directly observed on a real crystal under optical microscope. One particular \(\mathsf{P C N-700-M e}_{2}\) crystal was measured with a length of \(126\,\upmu\mathrm{m}\) along \(c\) -axis, which shrank to \(93\;\upmu\mathrm{m}\) (Figure 1a,b) upon desolvation ( \(c\) -direction is determined by Apex 2, Figure S1). These values match very well with unitcell parameters determined by SC-XRD. Such high elasticity in macroscopic single crystals is rarely observed to our knowledge.[10]
The SC-XRD investigation of the breathing mechanism manifests the structure-property correlations, which further enable us to judiciously modulate the flexibility of the PCN
700 system. Since \(\mathsf{P C N-700-M e}_{2}\) undergoes unit-cell dimensional contraction along \(c\) -axis, which involves a rotation of the \(\mathbf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{C}\) bond between phenyl rings, we speculate that the framework flexibility can be tuned by changing substituents on phenyl rings of the linker. To tune the breathing amplitude, linkers with different substituents were selected, namely \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Me}_{4}\) -BPDC \((2,2^{\prime},6,6^{\prime})\) -tetramethylbiphenyl- \(.4,4^{\prime}\) -dicarboxylic acid), \(\mathrm{H}_{2}(\mathrm{CF}_{3})_{2}\) -BPDC (2,2'-bis(trifluoromethyl)biphenyl\(4{,}4^{\prime}\) -dicarboxylatic acid), and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Me}\) -BPDC (2-methylbiphenyl- \(.4,4^{\prime}\) -dicarboxylic acid; Table 1). As expected, \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Me}_{4}\) - BPDC and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}(\mathrm{CF}_{3})_{2}\) -BPDC give rise to MOFs with increased
structural rigidity because of the elevated steric hindrance. Among this series, \(\mathsf{P C N-700-M e}_{4}\) exhibits the highest degree of rigidity while only small changes of \(c\) -axis length \((15.4\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\) to \(14.1\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}})\) and unit cell volume \((9008\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3}\) to \(8170\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3})\) were observed upon desolvation (Table 1 and Table S3). Intuitively, linkers with less bulky substituents tend to form more flexible MOF structures, as demonstrated in MIL-53 and MIL-88 systems.[11] However, this is not the case in PCN-700 system. The substituents on the phenyl rings are required to be bulky enough to induce two off-plane carboxylate groups. As far as steric hindrance is concerned, \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Me}\) -BPDC is expected to generate an even more flexible MOF than PCN\(700\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Me}_{2}\) . However, only an fcu net (UiO-67 analogue) was obtained by using \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{Me}\) -BPDC. \(\mathsf{P C N-700-M e}_{2}\) is by far, the most flexible MOF that we obtained with the fcu topology.
The breathing amplitude of \(\mathsf{P C N-700-M e}_{2}\) can also be manipulated through linker installation. It has been proved that dicarboxylate linkers with different lengths can be installed between \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\) clusters (Scheme S5).[12] Four linear linkers with different lengths, namely FA (fumarate), BDC (1,4-benzenedicarboxylate), NDC (2,6-napthalene dicarboxylate), and BPDC (4,4'-biphenyldicarboxylate), were selected and installed in PCN-700 respectively. To carry out linker installation, \(\mathsf{P C N-700-M e}_{2}\) crystals were soaked in a DMF
solution of linear linker at \(75\,^{\circ}\mathbf{C}\) for \(24\,\mathrm{{h}}\) . The subsequently installed linkers are unambiguously observed by SC-XRD. As illustrated in Figure 2a, the subsequently installed linkers support the MOF structure just like the "jack screws" support the scissor jack. The channel size along the \(a\) -axis directly correlates to the linker length as shown by SC-XRD (Figure 2b) and reinforced by PXRD patterns and \(\mathbf{N}_{2}\) isotherms (Figure S5–S32). It is worth pointing out that the linker installation can induce a much larger unit cell dimension change ( \(62\,\%\) contraction along \(c\) -axis) than the removal of solvent \(32\,\%\) contraction along \(c\) -axis), indicating that a larger breathing amplitude can be achieved by stronger guest–host interactions, such as coordination bonds (see Supporting Information for details).
The inherent cavities and dynamic behaviors of flexible MOFs allow the cavity environment to be tuned by external stimuli, which could in turn influence the catalytic activity of the encapsulated catalyst (Figure S38). Nevertheless, flexible MOFs for switchable catalysis have not been widely explored. This could be ascribed to the fact that the close-conformation of flexible MOFs can be opened up by solvent molecules under common catalytic conditions. In contrast to most flexible MOFs, \(\mathsf{P C N-700-M e}_{2}\) shows a very limited dependence on the solvents (Table S2). The desolvated PCN-700- \(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{e}_{2}\) maintains its shrunken structure in DMF at room temperature and is only reversed by treating with trifluoroacetic acid/DMF solution. These observations suggest that \(\mathsf{P C N-700-M e}_{2}\) solid can be considered as "rigid" under common catalytic reaction conditions. The closed conformation and opened conformation are expected to show different catalytic activity in the same solvent.
As a proof of concept, the cycloaddition reaction of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) with epoxides was selected to evaluate the Lewis-acid catalytic activity of \(\mathsf{P C N-700-M e}_{2}\) .[14] The performances of as-synthesized \(\mathsf{P C N-700-M e}_{2}\) (denoted as PCN-700-o, stands for open conformation) and desolvated \(\mathsf{P C N-700-M e}_{2}\) (denoted as PCN-700-c, stands for closed conformation) samples were evaluated with different epoxides at \(50\,^{\circ}\mathbf{C}\) under 1 atm \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) pressure and solvent-free condition. As shown in Table 2, the desolvated sample, PCN-700-c, shows prominently decreased performance. Yet, the catalytic activity of PCN-700-c can be restored by trifluoroacetic acid/DMF treatment which expands the shrunken structure to the
original configuration. The catalytic activity of \(\mathsf{P C N-700-M e}_{2}\) was turned on and off for four times, demonstrating a successful reversible control of the catalytic activity towards \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) fixation reaction (Figure S42).
To elucidate the mechanism of the switchable catalytic activity, control experiments were further conducted. The difference in catalytic activity of PCN-700-o and PCN-700-c could be possibly attributed to 1) different diffusion rates of substrate and 2) different Lewis acid sites. To eliminate the influence of substrate diffusion, \(V_{\mathrm{max}}\) was mea
sured which represents the maximum rate achieved by catalysts at saturating substrate concentrations. \(V_{\mathrm{max}}\) of PCN-700-o and PCN-700-c was calculated to be 42.9 and \(6.95\;\mathrm{mM}\,\mathrm{min}^{-1}\) respectively, showing a clear difference in maximum reaction velocity. Usually, \(V_{\mathrm{max}}\) depends on the efficiency and concentration of active sites, which is believed to be the - \(\cdot\mathrm{OH^{-}/H_{2}O}\) groups on the \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\) clusters.[13] To prove this, control experiments were carried out under the same condition using UiO-67 as a Lewis acid, which has no accessible - \(\cdot\mathrm{OH^{-}/H_{2}O}\) groups on the clusters. The \(V_{\mathrm{max}}(\mathrm{UiO}-\) 67) was tested to be \(4.54\,\mathrm{mM}\,\mathrm{min}^{-1}\) , which is about 10 times smaller than that of \(V_{\mathrm{max}}(\mathrm{PCN-700-o})\) , indicating the prominent influence of - \(\cdot\mathrm{OH^{-}/H_{2}O}\) groups. In PCN-700 structure, there are two pairs of symmetrically independent - \(\cdot\mathrm{OH^{-}/H_{2}O}\) groups on one \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\) cluster along \(c\) -direction and \(b\) -direction, respectively. The - \(\cdot\mathrm{OH^{-}/H_{2}O}\) groups along \(c\) -direction and \(b\) - direction in PCN-700-o are both accessible by substrates (Figure 3a), whereas - \(\cdot\mathrm{OH^{-}/H_{2}O}\) groups in PCN-700-c are only accessible from the \(b\) -direction, because of the close
packing of ligand and clusters along the \(c\) -direction, leaving no room for substrates (Figure 3b).
The halved active site in PCN-700-c is expected to reduce the \(V_{\mathrm{max}}\) by half. However, the experimental data suggested that the \(V_{\mathrm{max}}\) (PCN-700-c) is actually reduced by \(84\,\%\) compared to that of \(V_{\mathrm{max}}\) (PCN-700-o). We propose that the -OH /H2O groups along \(c\) -direction are more active than those along \(b\) -direction, thus burying the -OH /H2O groups along \(c\) -direction caused a dramatic decrease in catalytic activity for PCN-700-c. Indeed, we observed that the -OH / \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) groups along \(c\) -direction selectively deprotonate to react with basic metal hydroxides, whereas the -OH /H2O groups along the \(b\) -direction tend to act as bases to react with molybdic acid (Figure S46).[14] To further support our hypothesis, we used density functional theory (DFT) to calculate the relative identities of \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\) models of PCN-700-o and PCN-700-c (see Supporting Information, Section S9). The calculated \(\Delta\mathrm{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}\) value for the PCN-700-o- \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\) model (the \(\mathrm{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}\) of -OH / \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) along the \(b\) -direction minus the one along the \(c\) - direction) is 0.56. Similarly, this \(\Delta\mathrm{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}\) value for the PCN700-c- \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\) model is 2.82. The different acidity in the two directions can be tentatively attributed to the unequal electron distribution resulting from the asymmetric organic linkers. Consistent with this explanation, the asymmetric character is more notable in PCN-700-c than that in PCN-700- o, which explains the larger \(\Delta\mathrm{p}K_{\mathrm{a}}\) value of PCN-700-c- \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\) than that of PCN-700-o- \(Z\mathbf{r}_{6}\) . Thus, the remarkable decrease of acidity along the \(b\) -direction, as well as the only availability of the \(b\) -direction for catalysis in PCN-700-c, results in a much lower catalytic activity of PCN-700-c than that of PCN-700-o. In short, upon removal of solvent, \(\mathrm{PCN-700-Me}_{2}\) experiences a dramatic shrinkage along \(c\) -axis which changes the cavity environment and catalytic activity.
In summary, we have conducted a comprehensive study on flexible Zr-MOFs as switchable catalysts. Single-crystal Xray diffraction was employed to study the mechanism of the structural transformation. Organic linkers with different functional groups were utilized to rationally adjust the framework flexibility. A linker installation strategy was exploited to magnify the breathing amplitude. Furthermore, the activity of PCN-700-Me2 as a Lewis acid catalyst can be turned on and off by structural breathing, making it a switchable catalyst. We believe that the concept of switchable
catalysis within flexible MOF systems will not only lead to a new generation of catalysts, but also open up a field of study intersecting with both crystalline porous materials and switchable catalysts.
